    "The debate on France's budget for 2027 has formally begun in the National Assembly. On the evening of Tuesday, the 13th of October, deputies first had to decide whether to examine the bill at all. The Socialist group had tabled a motion to reject it before any discussion. That motion was defeated by 236 votes to 149. The left voted in favour, while the centrist bloc, the Republicans and the National Rally voted against, allowing the debate to go ahead. If the motion had passed, the bill would have been sent directly to the Senate.\n\nThe first part under discussion is the revenue section, which sets taxes and the overall balance of public finances. On the 9th of October, the finance committee had rejected this section by 31 votes to 3. Committee votes are only advisory, so the chamber started again from the government's original text. The government of Prime Minister Sebastien Lecornu has no absolute majority. It aims to bring the public deficit down to 5 percent of gross domestic product in 2027, after several years of high deficits and rising debt costs.\n\nThe Public Accounts Minister, David Amiel, described the text as a rescue budget and said deputies could discuss how savings are made, but not the total amount. The Economy Minister, Roland Lescure, said the government was approaching the debate in a spirit of compromise.\n\nWhat happens next? Debate on revenue was expected to continue until Monday, the 19th of October. Without a stable majority, the government may need deals with opposition groups, or could turn to special constitutional tools, such as article 49.3 or budget ordinances if Parliament fails to vote within 70 days.\n\nWhy does it matter? The budget decides taxes and spending for the whole country and tests whether a minority government can govern.\n\nTo recap: the rejection motion failed, and French deputies are now examining the 2027 budget line by line.",
